hey David won't medicare-for-all kill  jobs this is a really loaded question  and I'm glad to have the opportunity to  talk about it there's this imagery of  killing jobs that is a very political  imagery to be quite honest with you  because the reality is that all the time  governments federal government state  governments departments of the  government whoever all the time  decisions are being made that have an  economic impact and they have an  employment impact sometimes we're  talking about thousands we're talking  about a thousand new jobs but we lose  200 but there's some economic gain or  some social gain from that change so  number one we've got to get away from  this imagery of quote killing jobs  because all the time as a country  evolves decisions are made that create  jobs and that eliminate jobs they cause  firings they cause layoffs they create  new industries etc what we have to  understand is that a lot of those jobs  that exist in the medical field right  now which would be unnecessary and thus  would disappear under a medicare-for-all  system are not jobs that are actually  providing for the greater good what I  mean by that is if you consider medical  ensure health insurance fraud  investigators medical billing and coding  experts on the insurance side medical  billing and coding experts on the  hospital side by the way not all of them  would go away under single-payer under  many single-payer systems they remained  all of this bureaucracy plus the profit  that is implicit in the system that  doesn't lead to better health outcomes  it doesn't lead to lower costs it's  simply yes providing work to the people  that are in those jobs and it is work  that is being paid for at the expense of  people who are buying availing  themselves of health care services so  would some of those things be eliminated  if Medicare for all were to be passed  yeah some of them would the economy  would have to adjust in history this has  happened many many times I give this  example often and I think  applies here think back to the  development of the automobile the  automobile was a much more efficient  means of transportation than horse and  buggy carriages or whatever you call  them there were people who said this is  going to eliminate the jobs of the  drivers I don't know what they were  called of the horse buggies would that  be a reasonable reason not or not to  allow cars to be developed or to prevent  the development of automobiles of course  not  ultimately those individuals no longer  were in demand in that role of horse  buggy drivers they were reintegrated  into the workplace in other ways some of  them probably suffered long-term  unemployment that's true but we need to  stop falling for this idea that any  policy that will result in any job loss  is unacceptable in fact it's actually a  very sort of center-right idea I mean  there's this infamous video of Milton  Friedman arguing with some college kid  where the college kid says shouldn't  there have been consequences for some  car company that had a car that was  dangerous and Milton Friedman said okay  well hold on so there was a negative  consequence from the car from some kind  of negligence how much should that car  company have paid to fix that problem if  the car costs 10 grand  and it cost them 10 grand to fix their  problem should they have been forced to  do it  what about should they have paid  $100,000 to fix a problem on a $10,000  car at a certain point you get to this  realization that there are going to be  negative consequences to doing the right  thing and whether you run the Milton  Friedman side of that or not it doesn't  actually matter the point is every  single day decisions are being made  policy is being affected where some jobs  are lost there is some economic cost it  has to be accepted in a country of 340  million people anytime you make any kind  of change there will be people who gain  and people who lose right now the losers  are people who can't afford to pay for  care people who get substandard care  people who are going bankrupt because of  medical bills and those who gain are the  for-profit insurance companies the  elected officials  that perpetuate the status quo in  exchange for donations and whatever else  yes some jobs won't be necessary under  Medicare for all the net benefit is  gigantic and that or some system like it  is still the right thing even though  there will be jobs that will no longer  be necessary and we just have to accept  that and not fall for the right wing  talking point that it will kill jobs and  by the way that is a that is an on to  the defensive talking point when the  right goes to Medicare for all will kill  jobs they've often already conceded some  of the benefits of just such a system in  terms of access to care and in reducing  cost that's why they retreat to it and  it is a very very weak counterpoint to  some kind of non profit system the likes  of which many countries have already  employed let me know what you think we  have a great bonus show for you today  become a member or a patron to get  instant access at WWJ in pokémon comm or  at patreon.com slash david pakman show
 

